::ALG2_U04_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>4</sub>(ab). {
= log<sub>4</sub>(a) + log<sub>4</sub>(b)#Correct. Use the product property.
~ log<sub>4</sub>(a) - log<sub>4</sub>(b)#This is the quotient pattern.
~ log<sub>4</sub>(a + b)#This changes multiplication to addition inside one log.
~ log<sub>4</sub>(a/b)#This is not equivalent to a product.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>3</sub>(x/y). {
~ log<sub>3</sub>(x) + log<sub>3</sub>(y)#Addition corresponds to multiplication.
= log<sub>3</sub>(x) - log<sub>3</sub>(y)#Correct. Use the quotient property.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(x - y)#There is no property for subtraction inside the argument.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(y) - log<sub>3</sub>(x)#This reverses the quotient.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>6</sub>(x<sup>5</sup>). {
~ log<sub>6</sub>(5x)#The exponent becomes a coefficient, not a factor.
~ log<sub>6</sub>(x) + 5#This is not the power property.
= 5log<sub>6</sub>(x)#Correct. Use the power property.
~ log<sub>6</sub>(x)/5#This does not match exponent behavior.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense log<sub>2</sub>(m) + log<sub>2</sub>(n). {
~ log<sub>2</sub>(m/n)#Subtraction would make a quotient.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(m + n)#There is no sum property inside the argument.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(m - n)#This is not equivalent.
= log<sub>2</sub>(mn)#Correct. Same-base logarithms added condense to a product.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense log<sub>8</sub>(p) - log<sub>8</sub>(q). {
= log<sub>8</sub>(p/q)#Correct. Same-base logarithms subtracted condense to a quotient.
~ log<sub>8</sub>(pq)#This would match addition.
~ log<sub>8</sub>(p - q)#There is no subtraction property inside the argument.
~ log<sub>8</sub>(q/p)#This reverses the quotient.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense 2log<sub>5</sub>(x). {
~ log<sub>5</sub>(2x)#This turns the coefficient into a factor incorrectly.
= log<sub>5</sub>(x<sup>2</sup>)#Correct. The coefficient becomes an exponent.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(x + 2)#This is not a logarithmic property.
~ 2 + log<sub>5</sub>(x)#This changes multiplication into addition.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which property is used in log<sub>b</sub>(MN) = log<sub>b</sub>(M) + log<sub>b</sub>(N)? {
~ Quotient property#The quotient property involves subtraction.
~ Power property#The power property moves exponents.
= Product property#Correct. A product inside a log becomes a sum of logs.
~ Change of base#Change of base rewrites the base using a ratio of logs.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which property is used in log<sub>b</sub>(M/N) = log<sub>b</sub>(M) - log<sub>b</sub>(N)? {
~ Product property#The product property involves addition.
~ Power property#The power property moves exponents.
~ Change of base#Change of base rewrites the base.
= Quotient property#Correct. A quotient inside a log becomes a difference of logs.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which property is used in log<sub>b</sub>(M<sup>k</sup>) = klog<sub>b</sub>(M)? {
= Power property#Correct. The exponent becomes a coefficient.
~ Product property#The product property splits multiplication.
~ Quotient property#The quotient property splits division.
~ Change of base#Change of base changes the logarithm base.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite log<sub>3</sub>(10) using common logarithms. {
~ log(3)/log(10)#This reverses input and base.
= log(10)/log(3)#Correct. Change of base gives log(input)/log(base).
~ log(30)#This multiplies inside one log incorrectly.
~ log(10 - 3)#This is not change of base.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>2</sub>(3x<sup>4</sup>). {
~ log<sub>2</sub>(3) + log<sub>2</sub>(4x)#The exponent 4 should become a coefficient.
~ 4log<sub>2</sub>(3x)#The exponent applies only to x, not to 3x.
= log<sub>2</sub>(3) + 4log<sub>2</sub>(x)#Correct. Use product and power properties.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(3 + x<sup>4</sup>)#This changes multiplication to addition inside the argument.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense log<sub>4</sub>(a) + 3log<sub>4</sub>(b). {
~ log<sub>4</sub>(3ab)#The coefficient becomes an exponent, not a factor.
~ log<sub>4</sub>(a + b<sup>3</sup>)#Addition of logs becomes multiplication, not addition inside one log.
~ log<sub>4</sub>(a/b<sup>3</sup>)#Subtraction would create a quotient.
= log<sub>4</sub>(ab<sup>3</sup>)#Correct. Move 3 to an exponent, then use the product property.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense 2log<sub>7</sub>(x) - log<sub>7</sub>(y). {
= log<sub>7</sub>(x<sup>2</sup>/y)#Correct. Use the power property, then the quotient property.
~ log<sub>7</sub>(2x/y)#The coefficient 2 should become an exponent.
~ log<sub>7</sub>(x<sup>2</sup>y)#Subtraction creates division, not multiplication.
~ log<sub>7</sub>(y/x<sup>2</sup>)#This reverses the quotient.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to log<sub>5</sub>(x<sup>2</sup>y)? {
~ log<sub>5</sub>(2x) + log<sub>5</sub>(y)#The exponent 2 becomes a coefficient, not a factor.
= 2log<sub>5</sub>(x) + log<sub>5</sub>(y)#Correct. Use power and product properties.
~ 2log<sub>5</sub>(xy)#The exponent applies only to x.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(x<sup>2</sup>) - log<sub>5</sub>(y)#This would represent division by y.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to log<sub>9</sub>(m/n<sup>3</sup>)? {
~ log<sub>9</sub>(m) + 3log<sub>9</sub>(n)#This represents multiplication by n cubed.
~ 3log<sub>9</sub>(m) - log<sub>9</sub>(n)#The exponent 3 applies to n, not m.
= log<sub>9</sub>(m) - 3log<sub>9</sub>(n)#Correct. Use quotient and power properties.
~ log<sub>9</sub>(m - n<sup>3</sup>)#Subtraction inside a log is not equivalent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which rewrite is valid? {
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x + y) = log<sub>2</sub>(x) + log<sub>2</sub>(y)#There is no sum property.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x - y) = log<sub>2</sub>(x) - log<sub>2</sub>(y)#There is no subtraction-inside property.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x/y) = log<sub>2</sub>(y) - log<sub>2</sub>(x)#This reverses the quotient.
= log<sub>2</sub>(xy) = log<sub>2</sub>(x) + log<sub>2</sub>(y)#Correct. This is the product property.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which rewrite is invalid? {
= log<sub>3</sub>(a + b) = log<sub>3</sub>(a) + log<sub>3</sub>(b)#Correct. There is no logarithm property for splitting addition inside the argument.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(ab) = log<sub>3</sub>(a) + log<sub>3</sub>(b)#This is valid by the product property.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(a/b) = log<sub>3</sub>(a) - log<sub>3</sub>(b)#This is valid by the quotient property.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(a<sup>2</sup>) = 2log<sub>3</sub>(a)#This is valid by the power property.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite log<sub>4</sub>(17) using natural logarithms. {
~ ln(4)/ln(17)#This reverses input and base.
= ln(17)/ln(4)#Correct. Change of base can use any consistent log base.
~ ln(68)#This multiplies input and base incorrectly.
~ ln(17 - 4)#This is not change of base.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense log<sub>2</sub>(x) + log<sub>2</sub>(y) - log<sub>2</sub>(z). {
~ log<sub>2</sub>(x + y - z)#Log properties do not preserve addition or subtraction inside the argument.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(xz/y)#This puts the quotient in the wrong order.
= log<sub>2</sub>(xy/z)#Correct. Addition gives multiplication and subtraction gives division.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(z/xy)#This reverses the full quotient.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Expand log<sub>6</sub>((a<sup>2</sup>b)/c). {
~ log<sub>6</sub>(2a) + log<sub>6</sub>(b) - log<sub>6</sub>(c)#The exponent should become a coefficient.
~ 2log<sub>6</sub>(ab) - log<sub>6</sub>(c)#The exponent applies only to a.
~ 2log<sub>6</sub>(a) - log<sub>6</sub>(bc)#This places b in the denominator incorrectly.
= 2log<sub>6</sub>(a) + log<sub>6</sub>(b) - log<sub>6</sub>(c)#Correct. Use power, product, and quotient properties.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>What must be true about inputs when using logarithmic properties? {
= Each logarithm input must be positive.#Correct. Logarithms are defined only for positive arguments.
~ Inputs may be zero.#Zero is not allowed as a logarithm input.
~ Inputs must be negative.#Negative arguments are not allowed in this Algebra II context.
~ Only the base must be positive.#The base has restrictions, but the argument must also be positive.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>2</sub>(8x) expanded? {
~ log<sub>2</sub>(8 + x)#This changes multiplication into addition inside one log.
= log<sub>2</sub>(8) + log<sub>2</sub>(x)#Correct. Use the product property.
~ 8log<sub>2</sub>(x)#The 8 is a factor, not an exponent on x.
~ log<sub>2</sub>(8) - log<sub>2</sub>(x)#Subtraction corresponds to division.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>10</sub>(100x<sup>2</sup>) expanded? {
~ 100log<sub>10</sub>(x<sup>2</sup>)#The factor 100 does not become a coefficient.
~ log<sub>10</sub>(100 + x<sup>2</sup>)#This changes multiplication to addition.
= log<sub>10</sub>(100) + 2log<sub>10</sub>(x)#Correct. Use product and power properties.
~ 2log<sub>10</sub>(100x)#The exponent applies only to x.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense log<sub>3</sub>(x) + log<sub>3</sub>(4). {
~ log<sub>3</sub>(x + 4)#This is not a valid log property.
~ log<sub>3</sub>(x/4)#This would match subtraction.
~ 4log<sub>3</sub>(x)#This means log of x to the fourth, not log of 4x.
= log<sub>3</sub>(4x)#Correct. Addition of same-base logs condenses to multiplication.
}

::ALG2_U04_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.4<br><br>Question:<br>Condense log<sub>5</sub>(x) - 2log<sub>5</sub>(y). {
= log<sub>5</sub>(x/y<sup>2</sup>)#Correct. Move 2 to an exponent, then use the quotient property.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(x - y<sup>2</sup>)#Subtraction of logs does not become subtraction inside one log.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(xy<sup>2</sup>)#Subtraction creates a quotient, not a product.
~ log<sub>5</sub>(y<sup>2</sup>/x)#This reverses the quotient.
}

